# Implementation Plan - Phase 9 (Subphase 9.4): Sentence-Boundary Streaming for Sub-Second Audio Latency

## Overview
Currently, the AI companion pipeline incurs high Time-to-First-Audio (TTFA) because it waits for the entire LLM response to complete and then synthesizes the entire response block with Edge-TTS before sending audio over WebSocket.

Subphase 9.4 introduces **token-level streaming and sentence-boundary chunking**:
1. Tokens stream asynchronously from the LLM via LangChain's `model.astream()`.
2. A sentence accumulator buffer detects natural sentence boundaries (`.`, `!`, `?`, `\n\n`) while filtering out abbreviations (`Dr.`, `e.g.`) and decimal numbers (`3.14`).
3. An asynchronous producer-consumer pipeline pipes each assembled sentence immediately into `edge_tts.Communicate`.
4. As each sentence finishes synthesizing, its audio bytes are dispatched over the WebSocket with streaming metadata (`chunk_index`, `accumulated_text`, `is_final`).
5. The frontend maintains an audio playback queue to seamlessly transition from sentence to sentence with zero audio stutter or overlap, while live subtitle text dynamically appends each sentence as it arrives.
6. Gesture interruption (open palm) cleanly aborts the LLM token stream, cancels any queued TTS synthesis, flushes the frontend audio buffer, and halts playback immediately.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **WebSocket Protocol Compatibility**: We preserve backward compatibility on the WebSocket event format. The event remains `type: "ai_audio_response"`, augmented with `chunk_index`, `accumulated_text`, and `is_final: boolean`.
> If an error or non-streamed response occurs, `is_final: true` is sent on chunk 0.

> [!NOTE]
> **Frontend Audio Queue**: The frontend `HTMLAudioElement` can only play one audio stream at a time. The frontend will implement an internal audio chunk FIFO queue. When sentence 0 ends (`onended`), sentence 1 starts immediately, ensuring continuous speech without interruption.

